================================================================================================================
  THE CONFRONTATION -- this is a candidate law only if the measurement is consistent with it
================================================================================================================
  published ephemeris MEASUREMENT   Q2 = (1.6 +/- 1.8)e-27 s^-2 -- consistent with zero at 0.9 sigma
  LambdaCDM / GR prediction         Q2 = 0 EXACTLY (strong equivalence principle) -> 0.89 sigma.  GR PASSES.
  framework, canonical Q2 = -2.006e-26 s^-2   ->    12.0 sigma from the measurement
  framework, alt       Q2 = -2.185e-26 s^-2   ->    13.0 sigma from the measurement
  [FAIL] q2-12 THE DECIDING CHECK, and it is written so it CAN fail against the framework: for K1 to be a candidate second law the predicted Q2 must be consistent with the ephemeris measurement at <3 sigma. It is not.  Route A is the RAR-fitting kernel and it is the one the Solar System excludes.   (canonical 12.0 sigma, alt 13.0 sigma -- both EXCLUDED)

  ---- convention, stated openly ----------------------------------------------------------------
  Desmond, Hees & Famaey 2024 write Q2 = (3/2) a0^{3/2}/sqrt(GM) * q_D with q_D ~ 0.21-0.27, i.e. their
  coefficient is 1.5x this script's q.  In their normalisation the same integral gives Q2 = 3.01e-26 s^-2, matching their published ~2.9e-26 and Hees+2016's 3.5-4.4e-26 to ~20%.
  The conclusion is convention-independent: BOTH normalisations are an order of magnitude above the
  measurement, so the verdict does not turn on the factor 1.5.  Reported both ways deliberately.

================================================================================================================
  VERDICT
================================================================================================================
  NOT a second Kepler-grade law, on TWO of the five criteria, and it is a LIABILITY on the data:
   (4) 'nobody has stated it' FAILS.  Milgrom 2009 (MNRAS 399, 474) posed the Solar-System EFE
       quadrupole; Blanchet & Novak 2011 (MNRAS 412, 2530) computed q for the standard families;
       Hees, Folkner, Jacobson & Park 2014 (PRD 89, 102002) and Hees et al. 2016 (PRD 93, 084018)
       set the ephemeris limit; Desmond, Hees & Famaey 2024 (MNRAS 530, 1781) turned it into the
       RAR-vs-Cassini incompatibility at 8.7 sigma.  The law-FORM is fully credited elsewhere.
   (4b) It is also NOT new to this programme.  real_research/CASSINI_QUADRUPOLE_CONSTRAINT.md
       (2026-06-05) already records Q2 ~ 3-5e-26 s^-2 and '~15-25 sigma over the central value'.
       What IS new here is only the full integral for the Route A kernel (the prior repo script,
       reviews/cassini_quadrupole_framework.py, says in its own docstring that it does an
       order-of-magnitude check and NOT the quadrupole integral).  A number, not a law.
   (3) 'holds across many systems' FAILS.  There is exactly one system with a measured Q2.
   (1),(2),(5) PASS: measured quantities, predicted coefficient, not a restatement (check q2-2).

  AND THE SIGN OF THE RESULT IS AGAINST THE FRAMEWORK: Q2 = -2.01e-26 (canonical) / -2.19e-26 (alt) s^-2
  against a measurement of (1.6 +/- 1.8)e-27 -- 12 sigma / 13 sigma.  In Desmond's
  normalisation, 17 / 19 sigma.  GR + cold dark matter, which predicts exactly zero, sits at 0.9 sigma.
  The proposing agent's 'high promise' rating is not supportable: the one candidate in this batch with a
  perfectly clean Upsilon lever is clean because it is a Solar-System test, and the Solar System is where
  this class of theory is in the most trouble.  Zero Upsilon leverage bought zero protection.

  ONE THING THAT SURVIVES, and it is worth recording: check q2-8.  The kernel ordering is
  standard < Route A < simple in |q|, i.e. Route A does NOT escape by being the sharpest kernel --
  its e^-sqrt(y) tail is sharp at LARGE y, but the quadrupole integral is dominated by x ~ 1 (r ~ r_M ~
  8000 AU) where every kernel looks alike.  The natural hope that a sharper transition threads Cassini
  is refuted for this kernel by direct computation, not by argument.

